comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-13T21:18:50.542Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence: shipped. applyReadOutputIncludeModes and readOutputIncludeModeOptions added to src/sdk/read-output-contracts.ts split canonical include tokens into declared projection modes and residual field selectors, deriving the mode set from the surface's own include legacy_aliases minus the two value-bearing ones. Both dispatch paths forward consumed modes to the command before it computes rows: src/cli/main.ts writes them back through Commander (the resolved-global cache is cleared and re-read before the handler runs, so stripping only the local copy was not enough) and src/sdk/runtime.ts mirrors it for MCP actions. rejectEmptyIncludeProjection now refuses any projection that strips every field from every row, with the exit code and legal-domain wording get already used.\n\nVerification, all 16 declared (surface, mode) pairs: canonical output is byte-identical to the legacy alias after removing the receipt that records which spelling was used. list brief/compact/full, search compact/full, get full, history compact/full, activity compact/full, contracts full/summary, validate counts, deps full/summary, events full. Before the change 10 of 18 probed pairs returned the requested row count as empty objects with exit 0.\n\nRefusals now teach: 'pm context --output-include brief' returns exit 2 naming that context declares no projection modes; 'pm list --output-include nosuchfield' returns exit 2 naming brief, compact, full. Field selectors are unchanged ('--output-include id,title'), and a mixed request composes correctly (mode applied first, then field narrowing).\n\nFull suite green: 7,272 tests across 491 files. sdk/public-surface.json regenerated; both new symbols classify as contract_data, so the primitives are publicly reachable from the SDK."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-13T21:19:11.593Z","harness:claude-code","A compatibility layer that maps two different kinds of flag onto one canonical option inherits the obligation to implement both kinds. Here flagSelector() reduced --brief and --fields to the same shape — a bare token — while only one of them was ever a field name, and the difference was invisible because both spellings type-check as a selector string. The tell was that exactly one surface refused: get enumerates its legal selectors, so it could tell a mode from a field; every other surface had no enumeration and therefore no refusal. A layer that cannot enumerate its own legal domain cannot detect the case it does not handle."
files[3]{path,scope,note}:
  src/cli/main.ts,project,CLI dispatch forwards consumed modes to Commander before the handler runs
  src/sdk/read-output-contracts.ts,project,"Include-mode resolution,mode-to-option mapping,and the empty-projection refusal"
  src/sdk/runtime.ts,project,MCP action dispatch applies the same mode forwarding
